-id "q-51208" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\ln 2 - 4e^{x}$, find $f^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id 17592186237849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f85df34-ee93-5aca-8d12-bcf5fe3faa9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡5" :db/id 17592186236117} {:answer/id #uuid "4d519265-840b-5e03-a09d-732553549f65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186236310} {:answer/id #uuid "5283baad-0cc3-5be9-a599-fc7057b5e7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186236339} {:answer/id #uuid "5848a3b7-55a7-59c9-957c-d8c9f1aede42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186236373} {:answer/id #uuid "81168510-58be-58b5-8480-660231a90ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186236621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5848a3b7-55a7-59c9-957c-d8c9f1aede42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186236373} :answer-field/id #uuid "e280c21e-3772-51fb-a46a-1fbd0e6c47ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "987874a9-79ce-51e5-b80a-f5a44c3f0800" :question/math-academy-id "q-101587" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=5{e}^{x}+1$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $1$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(5{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =5{e}^{0} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186237850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d31247c-4643-5464-a30c-802705aaf50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186236007} {:answer/id #uuid "3b878615-e206-52a0-bf52-3f4f50ce31b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186236190} {:answer/id #uuid "54afb277-4dae-5d46-82a6-d2ef3ba15dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id 17592186236358} {:answer/id #uuid "9e126482-0792-53fb-9f81-3febb0cec7c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id 17592186236807} {:answer/id #uuid "a16f91bd-4ea9-5f5b-b438-3d185dbe8f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186236829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54afb277-4dae-5d46-82a6-d2ef3ba15dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id 17592186236358} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea4542f-4e0e-5b2c-a84d-fa40f33689f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf78a5ba-4334-5b57-bc16-3a848e2f0dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-101590" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-ln⁡11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186314904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cdb78ad-20bf-5af0-a094-1b3f6357e552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186312717} {:answer/id #uuid "6cffecb4-1fcc-411a-8289-8bcd57cb4ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6e^{x}" :db/id 17592186409337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cdb78ad-20bf-5af0-a094-1b3f6357e552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186312717} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a411b7f-e09b-54e6-9330-1c2c71a4efc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186314334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6869b95a-63a5-54db-b062-b375ad3ac68d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340056" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8,$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡8) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =6{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186314906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db64c05-bd48-51ef-9218-df180caf5534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id 17592186312840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db64c05-bd48-51ef-9218-df180caf5534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id 17592186312840} :answer-field/id #uuid "413c0c5a-e837-526a-8b03-fa256fc8b859" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186314186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19b7181e-4102-59d9-864a-c870a873bbda" :question/math-academy-id "q-340067" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡4$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡4) \\\\  & =9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =9{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡2} & ={(9{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9{e}^{ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186338886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2615d5d0-577d-51e9-a7eb-7299c88c1240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186336388} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4ee843-f5a5-5e6f-a7b4-d39ee29e9a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186336465} {:answer/id #uuid "935cffee-f030-54ce-8980-0ae960bd62a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=10-2{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186337376} {:answer/id #uuid "b4aa127c-c35b-5236-91c3-effa79e34c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=10-{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186337635} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f526af-9e12-5d00-a48a-09643a747fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=0" :db/id 17592186337899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d4ee843-f5a5-5e6f-a7b4-d39ee29e9a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186336465} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72ca8b2-1291-5b13-840e-71b284db3d8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd4ec376-93b4-54c8-9a98-7640cf3d729e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71053" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=10-2{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $10$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}
